<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- POGIL-style activity, Day 3 (~30 minutes): connecting rate of change to accumulated -->
<!-- change, and accumulated change to area. Builds toward definite integral notation -->
<!-- (as accumulated change, and as signed area) using only arithmetic and geometry -->
<!-- formulas, not antiderivatives yet. Continues the team-based format from Day 2. -->

<!-- Suggested pacing for a 30-minute period (approximate, not enforced): -->
<!--   Intro + Exercise 1 ......... ~4 min -->
<!--   Exercise 2 ................. ~5 min -->
<!--   Exercise 3 ................. ~5 min -->
<!--   Notation (two definitions) . ~2 min -->
<!--   Exercise 4 .................. ~6 min -->
<!--   Remark (signed area, beyond constant rates) . ~1.5 min -->
<!--   Exercise 5 .................. ~6.5 min -->

<worksheet xml:id="activity-accumulated-change-and-area">
  <title>From Rate to Accumulated Change: Distance, Area, and the Definite Integral</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-accumulated-change-and-area">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Compute a new position from a starting position, a constant rate of change, and elapsed time, and recognize that accumulating change over several time intervals means <em>adding</em> the increments from each one.</p></li>
      <li><p>Connect that arithmetic directly to geometry: the area between a constant-rate graph and the horizontal axis, computed as the sum of areas of simple regions.</p></li>
      <li><p>Read and interpret definite integral notation, both as accumulated change and as area.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain what <em>signed area</em> means, and why a negative rate of change corresponds to area below the axis.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use rectangle, triangle, and trapezoid area formulas to evaluate a definite integral of a piecewise linear rate function.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Today's activity connects two ideas that seem unrelated at first: adding up small
      increments of change to get a total change, and adding up small areas to get a total
      area. By the end of the activity you'll see these are the same idea in different
      clothes. And you'll learn the mathematical notation we use for it, the
      <em>definite integral</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Work with your team, using the same roles from last class. As always, the goal
      is for <em>everyone</em> in the group to be able to explain <em>why</em> an answer is
      correct, not just to have written one down.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Manager: Keep the team on task</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Recorder: Write down the answers the group agrees on</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Spokesperson: Speaks on behalf of the group</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Reflector: Observes participation and checks understanding of each participant</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout the activity, picture a small cart moving along a straight,
      meter-marked track. Its <em>position</em> <m>s</m> is measured in meters from a fixed
      zero mark (not necessarily where it starts), with the positive direction to the right. 
      Its <em>velocity</em> <m>v</m> is measured in meters per second (m/s): a positive 
      velocity means the cart moves right, a negative velocity means it moves left.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-single-rate" workspace="0in">
    <title>A Single Constant Rate</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Start with a relationship you already know.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-single-rate-compute" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At time <m>t=0</m> s, the cart is at position <m>s = 5</m> m. It moves at a constant
          velocity of <m>v = 3</m> m/s. Where is the cart at time <m>t = 4</m> s? Show the
          arithmetic that gets you there.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-single-rate-shifted-compute" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now do the same story, but the clock is shifted to a different starting time.
          At time <m>t=2</m> s, the cart is at position <m>s = 5</m> m. It moves at a constant
          velocity of <m>v = 3</m> m/s. Where is the cart at time <m>t = 6</m> s? Show the
          arithmetic that gets you there.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-single-rate-formula" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose you only know that the cart starts at some position <m>s_0</m> at time
          <m>t_0</m>, and then moves at a constant velocity <m>v</m> for the next
          <m>\Delta t</m> seconds. Write a formula for the cart's new position <m>s_1</m> at
          the later time <m>t_1 = t_0 + \Delta t</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-two-rates-arithmetic" workspace="0in">
    <title>Two Constant Rates, Back to Back</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Real motion rarely stays at one constant rate forever. Suppose the cart's velocity
        changes during the trip.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-two-rates-leg" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The cart starts at position <m>5</m> m at <m>t = 0</m>. For the first <m>4</m>
          seconds (<m>0 \lt t \lt 4</m>) it moves at <m>3</m> m/s, exactly as in Exercise 1. Then, for the next
          <m>3</m> seconds (<m>4 \lt t \lt 7</m>), it moves at a constant <m>2</m> m/s
          instead. How far does the cart travel during this second leg alone?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-two-rates-position" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the cart's position at <m>t = 7</m> s?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-two-rates-expression" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a single expression built from the two velocities, the two elapsed
          times, and the starting position that computes the position at <m>t=7</m>
          directly, without first finding the position at <m>t=4</m> as an intermediate step.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-two-rates-general" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Generalize: if a cart starts at position <m>s_0</m>, moves at rate <m>v_1</m> for a
          time span of length <m>\Delta t_1</m>, and then at rate <m>v_2</m> for a further time
          span <m>\Delta t_2</m>, write a general expression for its final position.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-two-rates-geometry" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Same Idea, Seen as Area</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Set the previous problem aside for a moment. Below is a graph of a velocity function
        <m>v(t)</m>, in m/s, over the first <m>7</m> seconds of some motion.
      </p>
      <image width="55%">
        <shortdescription>
          A step graph of velocity versus time: v(t) = 3 on the interval from t = 0 to t = 4,
          then v(t) = 2 on the interval from t = 4 to t = 7. Both rectangular regions between
          the graph and the t-axis are shaded.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=8cm, height=6cm,
            axis lines=middle,
            axis on top,
            xlabel={$t$ (seconds)}, ylabel={$v(t)$ (m/s)},
            xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
            xmin=-0.5, xmax=8.5, ymin=-1, ymax=5,
            xtick={4,7}, ytick={2,3},
            extra x ticks={0},
            clip=false,
          ]
            \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:4,3) -- (axis cs:4,0) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!30] (axis cs:4,0) -- (axis cs:4,2) -- (axis cs:7,2) -- (axis cs:7,0) -- cycle;
            \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:4,3);
            \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:4,2) -- (axis cs:7,2);
            \draw[thick,blue,dashed] (axis cs:4,3) -- (axis cs:4,2);
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-region1-shape" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What geometric shape is formed between the graph of <m>v(t)</m> and the <m>t</m>-axis
          on the interval <m>0 \le t \le 4</m>? Find its area, including units.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-region2-shape" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What shape is formed on the interval <m>4 \le t \le 7</m>? Find its area.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-regions-total" workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the combined (total) area of both regions.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-compare-to-exercise2" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare the areas you just found to your work in Exercise 2, specifically the
          expression you wrote (not counting the starting position <m>5</m>). What do you
          notice? State, in your own words, a general connection between the area under a
          velocity graph and the distance a cart travels.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <definition xml:id="def-integral-accumulated-change">
    <title>Notation: Accumulated Change</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What you just discovered works for any rate of change, not only velocity. If a
        quantity has value <m>s(t_1)</m> at time <m>t_1</m> and changes at rate <m>r(t)</m>,
        then its value at a later time <m>t_2</m> is the starting value plus the accumulated
        change over the interval. Mathematicians write that accumulated change using the
        <em>definite integral</em> symbol:
        <md>
          s(t_2) \, = \, s(t_1) \, + \, \int_{t_1}^{t_2} r(t)\, dt
        </md>
        Read <m>\int_{t_1}^{t_2} r(t)\,dt</m> as "the accumulated change in the quantity from
        <m>t_1</m> to <m>t_2</m>." When <m>r(t)</m> is a positive constant <m>v</m> on an
        interval of length <m>\Delta t</m>, this is just <m>v \cdot \Delta t</m> &#8212;
        exactly the rectangle areas you computed above.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-integral-area">
    <title>Notation: Area Under a Graph</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The very same symbol describes area. For a function <m>f(x)</m>, the area of the
        region between the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis, from <m>x=a</m> to
        <m>x=b</m>, is written
        <md>
          \int_a^b f(x)\, dx
        </md>
        This is not a coincidence: it's the same idea from Exercise 3, written in general
        notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-signed-area" workspace="0in">
    <title>What If the Rate Is Negative?</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now push on the idea. Suppose the cart's velocity is negative for part of the trip.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-signed-compute" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A cart starts at position <m>5</m> m and moves at <m>3</m> m/s for the first
          <m>4</m> seconds, exactly as before. For the next <m>3</m> seconds it moves at a
          constant <m>-2</m> m/s. What does a negative velocity mean physically for the cart?
          What is its position at <m>t = 7</m> s? Show your arithmetic.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-signed-graph" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The graph below shows this velocity function.
        </p>
        <image width="55%">
          <shortdescription>
            A step graph of velocity versus time: v(t) = 3 on the interval from t = 0 to
            t = 4, shaded above the t-axis, then v(t) = -2 on the interval from t = 4 to
            t = 7, shaded below the t-axis in a different color.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
              width=8cm, height=6cm,
              axis lines=middle,
              axis on top,
              xlabel={$t$ (seconds)}, ylabel={$v(t)$ (m/s)},
              xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=north west},
              xmin=-0.5, xmax=8.5, ymin=-3, ymax=5,
              xtick={4,7}, ytick={-2,3},
              extra x ticks={0},
              clip=false,
            ]
              \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:4,3) -- (axis cs:4,0) -- cycle;
              \fill[red!20] (axis cs:4,0) -- (axis cs:4,-2) -- (axis cs:7,-2) -- (axis cs:7,0) -- cycle;
              \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:4,3);
              \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:4,-2) -- (axis cs:7,-2);
              \draw[thick,blue,dashed] (axis cs:4,3) -- (axis cs:4,-2);
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-signed-region-area" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the interval <m>4 \le t \le 7</m>, the region between the graph and the axis lies
          below the <m>t</m>-axis. If you compute its area the ordinary way &#8212; treating
          base and height as positive lengths &#8212; what number do you get?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-signed-reconcile" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Add that number to the area of the first rectangle (from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=4</m>).
          Does the sum match the actual change in position you found above? If not, what
          adjustment makes it match?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-signed-rule" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State a rule: how should a region below the <m>t</m>-axis be counted when you use
          area to evaluate a definite integral?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-signed-area-and-beyond">
    <title>Signed Area, and Rates That Aren't Constant</title>
    <p>
      What you just worked out is called <em>signed area</em>: regions above the <m>t</m>-axis
      count as positive area, and regions below it count as negative area. This is exactly why
      the definite integral, and not just "area," is the right tool for accumulated change
      &#8212; it automatically keeps track of when a quantity is increasing versus decreasing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything above used constant rates, so every region was a rectangle. But the notation
      <m>\int_{t_1}^{t_2} r(t)\,dt</m>, and its meaning as signed area, works exactly the same
      way even when <m>r(t)</m> is not constant &#8212; even when its graph curves. Later in the
      course you'll learn algebraic techniques (the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus and
      antiderivative formulas) for evaluating such integrals exactly. For now, you have
      everything you need to evaluate a definite integral whenever the region between the
      graph and the axis is built from shapes you can already measure: rectangles, triangles,
      and trapezoids.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-piecewise-total" workspace="0in">
    <title>Putting It All Together</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The graph below shows the rate of change <m>r(t)</m> of some quantity over an
        <m>11</m>-second interval. It is piecewise <em>linear</em>: each piece is a straight
        line segment, so every region between the graph and the axis is a rectangle, a
        triangle, or a trapezoid. (Recall the trapezoid area formula
        <m>\frac{1}{2}(b_1+b_2)h</m>, using the two parallel sides <m>b_1</m>, <m>b_2</m> and
        the width <m>h</m> between them.)
      </p>
      <image width="65%">
        <shortdescription>
          A piecewise linear graph of r(t) versus t from t = 0 to t = 11. It is constant at 3
          from t = 0 to t = 2 (a rectangle region), rises linearly from 3 to 5 from t = 2 to
          t = 6 (a trapezoid region), falls linearly from 5 to 0 from t = 6 to t = 9 (a
          triangle region), then jumps to a constant -2 from t = 9 to t = 11 (a rectangle
          region below the axis). Regions above the axis are shaded blue; the region below is
          shaded red.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=10cm, height=7cm,
            axis lines=middle,
            axis on top,
            xlabel={$t$ (seconds)}, ylabel={$r(t)$},
            xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
            xmin=-0.5, xmax=13.5, ymin=-3.5, ymax=6.5,
            xtick={2,6,9,11}, ytick={-2,3,5},
            extra x ticks={0},
            clip=false,
          ]
            \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:2,3) -- (axis cs:2,0) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!25] (axis cs:2,0) -- (axis cs:2,3) -- (axis cs:6,5) -- (axis cs:6,0) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!35] (axis cs:6,0) -- (axis cs:6,5) -- (axis cs:9,0) -- cycle;
            \fill[red!20] (axis cs:9,0) -- (axis cs:9,-2) -- (axis cs:11,-2) -- (axis cs:11,0) -- cycle;
            \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:2,3) -- (axis cs:6,5) -- (axis cs:9,0);
            \draw[thick,blue] (axis cs:9,-2) -- (axis cs:11,-2);
            \draw[thick,blue,dashed] (axis cs:9,0) -- (axis cs:9,-2);
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-region1" workspace=".6in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interval <m>0 \le t \le 2</m>: name the shape, and find its signed area.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-region2" workspace=".6in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interval <m>2 \le t \le 6</m>: name the shape, and find its signed area.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-region3" workspace=".6in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interval <m>6 \le t \le 9</m>: name the shape, and find its signed area.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-region4" workspace=".6in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interval <m>9 \le t \le 11</m>: name the shape, and find its signed area. Be careful
          with the sign.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-sum" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Add up your four signed areas to find <m>\displaystyle\int_0^{11} r(t)\, dt</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-piecewise-application" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>r(t)</m> is the rate of change of some quantity whose value at <m>t=0</m>
          is <m>s(0) = 10</m>. Using the idea from this activity &#8212; not any formula you
          haven't learned yet &#8212; find <m>s(11)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
